.opencode/ export-ignore

# Keep LF line endings for templates and golden files to avoid
# cross-platform test failures (Windows CRLF conversion).
*.tmpl text eol=lf
*.golden text eol=lf
*.go text eol=lf
# The Go module files feed the same working-tree provenance digests as the
# sources above (provenance.Digest reads the checkout, not the blob), so a
# Windows CRLF checkout makes exportgen's oracle digest disagree with the pin.
go.mod text eol=lf
go.sum text eol=lf
# The detached Go oracle programs are carried as .txt so they do not compile
# with the generator; their bytes feed a provenance digest, so a Windows CRLF
# checkout would make that digest disagree with every other platform.
*.go.txt text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
Cargo.toml text eol=lf
Cargo.lock text eol=lf
rust-toolchain.toml text eol=lf
deny.toml text eol=lf
testdata/port/** text eol=lf
# The agent-skill contract fixtures are byte-compared through `include_str!`
# against the bytes the renderer produces, so a Windows CRLF checkout would
# make every comparison fail there.
crates/symvault-cli/tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf
# These embedded Cobra help pages are byte-for-byte golden output and are also
# parsed by the CLI, so keep their checkout bytes stable on Windows.
crates/symvault-cli/src/help-*.txt text eol=lf

# Store observation provenance hashes the committed normalization contract.
docs/rust-port/store-normalization.md text eol=lf
# Keep the embedded native Git test helper identical across checkouts.
crates/symvault-sync/tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

